You are modifying the existing Alva game repository (hiyroscript/alva).

This is the first platform-fighter conversion pass. Alva will eventually move toward the stage/combat presentation of games such as Brawlhalla, MultiVersus, and Super Smash Bros., but DO NOT redesign the whole combat system yet.

For this pass, make ONLY the stage, camera/FOV, 3D presentation, finite-stage, and Void changes described below.

Do not add stocks, percentages, new attacks, new movement mechanics, new characters, new menus, new HUD systems, or unrelated visual redesigns.

Existing architecture you must work with

Inspect the existing implementation before changing anything.

Important files/systems include:

* js/data/maps.js
    * Desert and City Quick Battle maps.
    * Desert is currently 3600 x 1000.
    * City is currently 3000 x 1100.
    * Both define bounds, cameraBounds, spawns, platforms/solids and a theme.
* js/data/practice-map.js
    * Practice Ground is separate from MAPS.
    * Currently 4000 x 1100.
    * It has its own bounds, cameraBounds, and spawn points.
* js/game/physics.js
    * StageCollision currently copies map.bounds.left/right.
    * stepBody() clamps fighters against stage.left/right.
    * separate() also clamps fighter pushboxes against those limits.
    * The current FLOOR / groundY implementation behaves as an effectively infinite horizontal floor. This MUST change or removing the side walls will only allow fighters to stand on invisible ground outside the stage.
* js/game/camera.js
    * Fixed-size world camera which follows/framing the two fighters and clamps against cameraBounds.
* js/game/arena.js
    * computeWorldScale() controls how zoomed-in gameplay appears.
    * The target fighter height currently comes from CONFIG.render.fighterScreenRatio.
* js/config.js
    * Current fighterScreenRatio is 0.16.
* js/stages/practice-theme.js
    * This is the visual reference for the new stage perspective.
    * It already creates a convincing pseudo-3D room using one-point perspective, FAR/NEAR depth factors, a projected floor, back wall, side walls and front face.
* js/stages/desert-theme.js
    * Currently uses layered 2D parallax.
    * Explicitly builds Boundary cliffs at the map bounds.
* js/stages/city-theme.js
    * Currently uses layered 2D parallax.
    * Explicitly builds Boundary buildings through buildBounds().
* js/game/projectile.js, js/game/clone.js, charged techniques and related tests also contain assumptions about horizontal stage bounds. Audit them when the old walls are removed.

Required changes

1. Convert all three stages from enclosed arenas into finite platform-fighter stages.

Desert, City, and Practice Ground must no longer have solid left/right arena walls.

Remove fighter collision clamping against the old map.bounds.left/right.

A fighter must be able to:

* run off either edge of the main stage;
* jump off the stage;
* fall below the stage;
* be knocked horizontally beyond the stage;
* attempt to recover before reaching the Void.

Do NOT replace the current walls with invisible walls.

The existing main floor is effectively infinite because groundY always provides a landing surface. Refactor this so the primary floor has a finite horizontal extent.

Use a clean data-driven representation such as a finite main floor/platform range rather than scattering hard-coded checks through physics.

For example, a map may expose a main stage/floor structure describing:

* left edge;
* right edge;
* top Y;
* visual thickness/depth.

The exact schema is your choice, but physics and visuals should share the same source of truth.

StageCollision.surfaceBelow() and stepBody() must only treat the main floor as valid support when the fighter horizontally overlaps the finite stage surface.

Preserve existing one-way platforms and solid obstacle behavior.

Update pushbox separation so fighters are no longer forced back inside former arena walls.

2. Make the physical stages substantially smaller.

The current stages are extremely wide for a platform fighter.

Shrink the actual fighting surfaces and reposition their contents around the middle of the map.

Target the general scale of a compact 1v1 platform-fighter stage rather than a long side-scrolling arena.

A reasonable starting target is approximately:

* Desert main fighting surface: ~1400–1600 world units wide.
* City main fighting surface: ~1400–1600 world units wide.
* Practice main fighting surface: ~1300–1500 world units wide.

These are tuning targets, not a reason to distort existing gameplay objects. Adjust them slightly if necessary so platforms, fighters and art remain coherent.

Reposition spawn points around the new center.

For City, compress/reposition the current seven one-way platforms into the smaller layout while preserving the stage’s vertical character.

For Desert, keep its relatively open identity.

For Practice, keep the simple training-stage identity.

Do not remove City platforms or Desert’s existing rock obstacles unless necessary to make the finite-stage geometry work.

3. Remove all three stages’ side-wall artwork.

This means both gameplay walls and visual walls.

Desert:

Remove the existing boundary cliffs generated by buildCliffs() as stage-edge walls. The desert should no longer look like a basin trapped between two enormous cliffs.

City:

Remove the left/right Boundary buildings created by buildBounds(). Buildings can remain in the background scenery, but nothing should visually read as a giant wall preventing the fighter from leaving the stage.

Practice Ground:

Remove the left/right side-wall and bulkhead treatment produced by drawSideWall() and drawBulkhead().

Keep the Practice Ground’s back-wall grid/perspective aesthetic. The instruction is to remove the enclosing SIDE walls, not flatten the training room into a blank background.

4. Zoom the gameplay camera out for a platform-fighter feel.

The current fighter target is roughly 16% of viewport height, which feels too close for the direction of the game.

Adjust the world scale so fighters generally occupy approximately 10–12% of viewport height during normal gameplay.

Use this as a tuning target rather than blindly applying a single number if it produces bad results on different aspect ratios.

The player should see:

* the entire core fighting platform most of the time;
* substantial air space above it;
* meaningful space past the left/right ledges;
* enough space underneath to visually understand falling/recovery;
* both fighters comfortably when they separate.

Do NOT zoom so far out that fighters become unreadable.

Keep responsive behavior working on desktop and smaller displays.

Do not implement a huge new cinematic camera system in this pass. Improve the existing Camera/scale system enough to establish the wider platform-fighter FOV.

Camera bounds should no longer be synonymous with the old solid arena walls.

5. Make Desert and City feel 3D like Practice Ground.

Stay within the existing Canvas 2D rendering architecture. Do NOT introduce Three.js, WebGL, a new engine, or external 3D assets.

The goal is pseudo-3D perspective like practice-theme.js.

Extract/reuse perspective concepts from Practice Ground where useful instead of duplicating large amounts of projection math.

The playable terrain should visibly have depth.

For Desert:

Turn the playable desert surface into a perspective sandstone/sand platform rather than a flat 2D strip.

It should have:

* a readable top surface;
* visible front thickness/face;
* perspective depth;
* shading that makes the stage feel volumetric;
* existing desert sky, sun, mesas, dunes, dust and thematic colors preserved where appropriate.

The stage can visually resemble a raised sandstone formation or compact desert plateau with open air beyond its ledges.

The old boundary cliffs must not return as disguised walls.

Rock obstacles should also get enough top/front/side shading to match the new depth treatment.

For City:

Turn the playable rooftop into an actual dimensional rooftop slab.

Give it:

* a perspective top plane;
* visible front facade/thickness;
* appropriate side/front shading;
* volumetric treatment of catwalks, girders, scaffolds, rooftop structures and the bulkhead.

Keep the existing nighttime skyline, moon, neon, trains, searchlights and city identity.

The core playable geometry should now visually belong in the same pseudo-3D world as Practice Ground instead of looking like flat layered shapes.

Practice Ground is the perspective reference, but Desert and City must retain their own art direction. Do not simply put Practice Ground’s grid onto them.

6. Add the new kill boundary: Void.

Introduce a data-driven stage property for a kill/blast boundary called Void.

The Void must sit significantly beyond the playable stage so fighters have real off-stage space.

Do not place the kill boundary directly beside the ledge.

A fighter should be able to:

* leave the platform;
* travel some distance away from it;
* potentially recover;
* only die once they cross the distant Void boundary.

The Void should surround the meaningful play space rather than only replacing the old left/right walls. It should account for falling below the stage and being launched far horizontally. A sufficiently distant upper kill boundary should also exist so extremely high launch positions cannot continue forever.

Use explicit per-stage voidBounds or an equivalent clean data structure.

Keep the Void boundary independent from:

* the finite platform edge;
* normal camera bounds;
* former wall bounds.

7. Void visuals.

The Void must have a visible visual identity.

It is a deep/pure black region with a wavy animated inner edge.

It should feel ominous and organic rather than like a straight black rectangle.

Requirements:

* call the system/region Void;
* use black as its dominant visual;
* render a gently wavering boundary;
* the wavy motion should be visual only;
* gameplay death detection must use a stable mathematical boundary, NOT the animated wave position;
* respect reduced-motion mode by freezing or greatly reducing wave animation;
* keep the Void far enough away that it does not visually crowd the stage during normal neutral play.

The Void should appear as fighters approach the blast-zone area rather than making every stage look boxed in by four black walls at all times.

It may be implemented as black regions outside the configured safe rectangle with sine/noise-style wavering inner edges.

Do not use external textures for this.

8. Void gameplay behavior.

Crossing the configured Void boundary is an immediate kill.

Do not add a stock system yet.

For Quick Battle, implement the minimum clean behavior necessary so entering the Void clearly counts as that fighter being defeated rather than allowing them to continue moving outside the world.

A Void death should immediately resolve the appropriate fighter as defeated. Do not make the player wait until the 99-second timer expires.

Reuse the existing battle/result architecture where sensible rather than building a full new match rules system.

For Practice Ground, Void contact must not permanently end the training session. Reset the affected fighter cleanly back to its appropriate spawn so Practice remains usable.

When resetting from Void:

* clear invalid velocity;
* clear transient attack/technique state that cannot survive a respawn;
* do not leave clones, projectiles, binds, or references pointing at an invalid/dead fighter.

Do not introduce stocks, respawn invulnerability, percentages or platform-fighter damage scaling yet.

9. Audit old wall-bound assumptions.

Removing stage.left/right as walls can affect systems other than walking.

Audit at minimum:

* fighter movement;
* fighter pushbox separation;
* clone placement;
* projectile cleanup;
* charged techniques that check ground or stage margins;
* camera clamping;
* stage previews;
* Practice Ground tests;
* any test that expects a fighter to stop at map.bounds.left/right.

Projectiles should eventually be culled using sensible world/Void limits rather than the old solid-wall boundary.

Clone placement should not behave as though invisible side walls still exist.

Do not break techniques that depend on checking whether ground exists under the fighter. Finite stage edges should correctly count as losing ground.

Camera and stage relationship

Keep these concepts separate:

Main platform
The physical surface fighters stand on.

Off-stage space
Open recoverable air beyond the ledges.

Camera framing area
Where the camera is allowed to travel/frame combat.

Void
The distant kill/blast boundary.

These four concepts must not be represented by one shared bounds rectangle anymore.

The existing architecture currently mixes several of these ideas. Clean that up enough for this pass.

